<<<
that these are correct, & that have cannot be omitted in the last two; but why? Because the word had in this phrase is not the mere auxiliary of mood or tense, but a true verb meaning find; You had better do it= You would find todo-it better;
===
that these are correct, & that *have* cannot be omitted in the last two; but why? Because the word *had* in this phrase is not the mere auxiliary of mood or tense, but a true verb meaning find; *You had better do it*=You would find to-do-it better;
>>>

<<<
You had better have done it=You would find to-havedone-it better. The sense is a little strained with transfer to passives (It had better have been done=It would find to-have-been-done better), since it is in strictness the doer,
===
*You had better have done it*=You would find to-have-done-it better. The sense is a little strained with transfer to passives (*It had better have been done*=It would find to-have-been-done better), since it is in strictness the doer,
>>>

